<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sacred Ensembles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, inscribed in 2023. It comprises three temples of the 12th and 13th centuries: the Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>, the Hoysaleswara temple at Halebidu and the Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somanathapura">Somanathapura</a>, the finest examples of Hoysala architecture and sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacred-ensembles-of-the-hoysalas.jpg" alt="Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dineshkannambadi at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Somanathapura_Keshava_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Two of the temples stand in Hassan district and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Belur lies about 35 kilometres north-west of the town of Hassan and about 16 kilometres from Halebidu, formerly Dorasamudra, the historical capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire</a>. Somanathapura lies 38 kilometres east of the city of Mysore. All three are built of chloritic schist, or soapstone, and raised on a jagati, a platform that follows the star-shaped outline of the shrine and served as a path for circumambulation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Hoysala dynasty ruled much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> between the 11th and 14th centuries. As they built up revenue and administration, its kings sought a distinct identity for their kingdom through patronage of art, architecture and literature, and their craftsmen developed what the art historian Adam Hardy calls the Karnata Dravida tradition. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a>, who came to power in 1110, commissioned the Chennakeshava temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> in 1117 with his queen Shantala; the complex kept growing for over 100 years. The Hoysaleswara temple, the largest dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, was commissioned by Ketamalla Dandanayaka, a merchant and court officer. The Keshava temple was founded by Somanatha Dandanayaka, a commander under King Narasimha III.</p><p>Armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> in the early 14th century, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> in 1326, plundered the temples. The Keshava temple was repaired in the 16th century with grants from the Vijayanagara emperors, as differences in the stonework still show.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The friezes of the plinths show elephants, lions and scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, and many sculptures carry their carvers' signatures. Halebidu's temple has twin shrines housing the Shivalingas Hoysalesvara and Shantalesvara; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somanathapura">Somanathapura</a>'s has three sanctums for forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> inside a gallery of 56 small shrines. The temples were declared monuments of national importance in 1951. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> remains a place of worship, while the other two are protected monuments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a>. The ensemble was inscribed under criteria (i) and (ii) in 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2023 they were inscribed together on the UNESCO World Heritage List as the <b>Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
